Copper Versus Optical Links — How Distance Changes the Connection

The big idea

Copper and optical links can carry the same nominal bit rate. The important difference is how far they can carry it with acceptable errors, power, density and cost.

A short electrical cable may be the simplest connection inside a rack. Stretch the same connection across rows, and high-frequency signal loss can make equalization harder, increase errors or make the link impossible to qualify. Optical fiber moves the long-distance part of the path into light, but it requires conversion hardware at the ends.

That makes media choice a physical design decision before it becomes a purchasing decision.

1. What travels through copper?

A copper link sends changing voltages and currents along conductors. The receiver must distinguish each symbol after the signal has been weakened and distorted by the cable, connectors and circuit-board traces.

As lane rate and distance rise, several problems become harder:

- Resistance and dielectric loss reduce high-frequency signal strength.
- Nearby conductors create crosstalk.
- Connectors and impedance changes reflect part of the signal.
- Noise and temperature reduce the receiver's remaining margin.

Transmitters use pre-emphasis, and receivers use equalization, to reconstruct the intended symbols. These techniques recover some margin, but they do not abolish physics.

A passive direct-attach copper cable, or DAC, contains conductors and connectors without powered signal processing in the cable. It is short, inexpensive and low-power. An active copper cable adds electronics that condition the signal, buying more reach at the cost of power, heat and complexity.

2. What travels through fiber?

An optical link begins electrically at the network interface or switch. A transmitter converts the electrical data into modulated light, fiber guides that light, and a photodetector converts it back to an electrical signal at the far end.

Fiber has much lower loss over useful data-center distances and does not suffer electrical crosstalk. It is also thinner and lighter than large bundles of high-speed copper. That helps when many links must pass through crowded racks and cable trays.

But “fiber” is not just glass. The complete link may include lasers or optical modulators, photodetectors, control electronics, connectors and sometimes a digital signal processor. Those components consume power, generate heat and must be packaged and tested.

An active optical cable, or AOC, permanently joins the optical ends to the fiber. Pluggable transceivers use removable optical modules and separate fiber. AOCs can simplify installation; pluggables can make module or fiber replacement easier. Neither form is automatically best.

3. Distance changes the answer

Imagine two 400 Gb/s links: one over a short DAC and one over optical fiber. Their advertised line rate is identical. Fiber has not made the bits intrinsically faster.

